Hello,

Short and to the point: I have built a desktop application for 3D
printing and would like to show it to you =E2=80=94 Solidon3D.

What probably makes it interesting for your channel: it does not take fit
clearances from a table. The user prints one small test piece once, and
from then on every part is built with the measured clearance of their own
filament. Everything else is non-destructive =E2=80=94 each step stays in the
history as an operation, so changing a dimension recomputes the twenty
steps that follow. Plus layer analysis with overhangs, islands and support
requirements. The AI chat is optional and is not allowed to compute
geometry; it can only trigger the same operations the menus expose.

The demo is complete and free, no account, usable until the end of
October: https://solidon3d.de/en/
Windows 10/11, around 173 MB; the macOS and Linux packages are following.

If you would like to take a look, I will happily set you up with a
permanent licence and put together a concrete task that shows the
difference. And if it is not right for your channel, that is entirely
fine =E2=80=94 I would appreciate two sentences on why not.

